window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-21"]=[{"id":"q3-73-74","q":"3:73–74","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:73–74 · The distribution of divine bounty and the safeguarding of spiritual knowledge","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 368–370","cairo":"I:368–370","source_page_first":368,"source_page_last":370,"ar":"وَلا تُؤْمِنُوا إِلاَّ لِمَنْ تَبِعَ دِينَكُمْ قُلْ إِنَّ الْهُدى هُدَى اللَّهِ أَنْ يُؤْتى أَحَدٌ مِثْلَ ما أُوتِيتُمْ أَوْ يُحاجُّوكُمْ عِنْدَ رَبِّكُمْ قُلْ إِنَّ الْفَضْلَ بِيَدِ اللَّهِ يُؤْتِيهِ مَنْ يَشاءُ وَاللَّهُ واسِعٌ عَلِيمٌ (73) يَخْتَصُّ بِرَحْمَتِهِ مَنْ يَشاءُ وَاللَّهُ ذُو الْفَضْلِ الْعَظِيمِ (74)\nقلت: يحتمل أن يكون قوله: (أن يؤتى) : مفعولاً ب- (تؤمنوا) ، و (قل إن الهدى هدى الله) : اعتراض، واللام في «لمن» صلة، (أو يحاجوكم) : عطف على (يؤتى) ، والتقدير: ولا تصدقوا أن يؤتى أحد مثل ما أوتيتم، إلا من كان على دينكم، ولا تصدقوا أن يحاجوكم عند ربكم، بل أنتم تحاجون غيركم. فردَّ الله عليهم (قل إن الهدى هدى الله) ، و (إن الفضل بيد الله) . ويحتمل أن يكون قوله: (أن يؤتى) مفعولاً لأجله، والعامل فيه محذوف، والتقدير: أدبَّرتم ما دبرتم كراهية أن يوتى أحد ما أوتيتم، ومخافة أن يحاجوكم عند ربكم؟.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله، حاكياً عن اليهود: (و) قالوا لا تُؤْمِنُوا أي: لا تقروا، أو تصدقوا أَنْ يُؤْتى أَحَدٌ مِثْلَ ما أُوتِيتُمْ من العلم والحكمة وفلق البحر وسائر الفضائل، إِلَّا لِمَنْ تَبِعَ دين اليهودية، وكان على دِينَكُمْ، ولا تؤمنوا أن يُحاجُّوكُمْ عِنْدَ رَبِّكُمْ لأنكم أصح ديناً منهم. قال الحق جل جلاله: قُلْ لهم: إِنَّ الْهُدى هُدَى اللَّهِ يهدي به مَن يشاء، وإِنَّ الْفَضْلَ بِيَدِ اللَّهِ يُؤْتِيهِ مَنْ يَشاءُ.\nأو يقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وقالوا: لا تصدقوا ولا تذعنوا إِلَّا لِمَنْ تَبِعَ دِينَكُمْ وكان من جلدتكم، فإن النبوة خاصة بكم. فكذبهم الحق بقوله: قُلْ إِنَّ الْهُدى هُدَى اللَّهِ، يخص بها مَن يشاء من عباده، فكيف تحصرونها فيكم؟ لأجل أَنْ يُؤْتى أَحَدٌ مِثْلَ ما أُوتِيتُمْ قلتم ما قلتم، ودبرتم ما دبرتم، حسداً وبغياً، (أو) خوفاً أن يُحاجُّوكُمْ عِنْدَ رَبِّكُمْ، يغلبوكم بالحجة لظهور دينهم، قُلْ يا محمد: إِنَّ الْفَضْلَ بِيَدِ اللَّهِ يُؤْتِيهِ مَنْ يَشاءُ فلا ينفع في رده حيلة ولا خدع.\nأو يقول الحق جلّ جلاله، للمؤمنين، تثبيتاً لهم وتشجيعاً لقلوبهم: ولا تصدقوا يا معشر المؤمنين أن يعطي أحد مثل ما أوتيتم من الفضل والدين القويم إلا من تبع دينكم الحق، وجاء به من عند الحق، ولا تصدقوا أن يحاجوكم في دينكم عِنْدَ رَبِّكُمْ، أو يقدر أحد على ذلك، فإن الهدى هدى الله والفضل بيد الله، يُؤْتِيهِ مَنْ يَشاءُ وَاللَّهُ واسِعٌ الفضل والكرم، عَلِيمٌ بمن يستحق الخصوصية والفضل، يَخْتَصُّ بِرَحْمَتِهِ مَنْ يَشاءُ كالنبوة وغيرها، وَاللَّهُ ذُو الْفَضْلِ الْعَظِيمِ لا حصر لفضله، كما لا حصر لذاته.\nالإشارة: يقول الحق- جلت ذاته، وعظمت قدرته- لأهل الخصوصية: ولا تقروا بالخصوصية إلا لمن كان على دينكم وطريقكم، وتزيّاً بزيكم، وبذل نفسه وفلسه في صحبتكم، مخافة أن يؤتى أحد مثل ما أوتيتم من الخصوصية، وهو ليس أهلاً لها، فيأخذها علماً، فإما أن يتزندق أو يتفسق، أو يحاجوكم بالشريعة فيريق دماءكم كما وقع للحلاج رضي الله عنه وفي ذلك يقول الشاعر:\nومن شَهِدَ الحَقِيقَةَ فَلْيَصُنْهَا ... وإلا سوف يقتل بالسنان\nكحلاج المحبة إذ تبدت ... له شمس الحقيقة بالتداني(1)\nوقال آخر:\nبالسِّرِّ إنْ بَاحُوا تُبَاحُ دِمَاؤُهُمْ ... وكَذَا دماء البائحين تباح\nوقل أيها العارف، لمن طلب الخصوصية قبل شروطها أو أنكر وجودها عند أهل شرطها: إن الهدى هُدَى اللهِ يهدي به مَن يشاء، والفضل بيد الله يُؤتيه مَن يشاء، والرحمة- التي هي الخصوصية- في قبضة الله، يخص بها مَن يشاء، (والله ذو الفضل العظيم) فمن أراد الخصوصية فليطلبها من معدنها، وهم العارفون بها، فيبذل نفسه وفلسه لهم حتى يُعرفوه بها. وبالله التوفيق.\nثم ذكر الحق- تعالى- وصف اليهود بالخيانة، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَلا تُؤْمِنُوا إِلاَّ لِمَنْ تَبِعَ دِينَكُمْ قُلْ إِنَّ الْهُدى هُدَى اللَّهِ أَنْ يُؤْتى أَحَدٌ مِثْلَ ما أُوتِيتُمْ أَوْ يُحاجُّوكُمْ عِنْدَ رَبِّكُمْ قُلْ إِنَّ الْفَضْلَ بِيَدِ اللَّهِ يُؤْتِيهِ مَنْ يَشاءُ وَاللَّهُ واسِعٌ عَلِيمٌ (73) يَخْتَصُّ بِرَحْمَتِهِ مَنْ يَشاءُ وَاللَّهُ ذُو الْفَضْلِ الْعَظِيمِ (74)","quran_en":"73. “Believe only one who follows your religion.” Say: “Guidance is God’s guidance.” “[Do not believe] that anyone is given the like of what you were given, or that they might dispute with you before your Lord.” Say: “Bounty is in God’s hand; He gives it to whom He wills. God is all-encompassing, knowing.” 74. He singles out for His mercy whom He wills. God is the possessor of immense bounty.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The disputed construction of an yuʾtā and the parenthetical reply","html":"<p>I say: “that anyone is given” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أن يؤتى<\/bdi>) may be the object (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مفعول<\/bdi>) of “believe” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تؤمنوا<\/bdi>), while “Say: ‘Guidance is God’s guidance’” is a parenthesis (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اعتراض<\/bdi>). The lām in li-man (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لمن<\/bdi>) is an additional linking particle (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">صلة<\/bdi>), and “or that they might dispute with you” is coordinated with yuʾtā. The reconstructed sense (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التقدير<\/bdi>) is: do not believe that anyone is given the like of what you were given, except someone of your own religion; and do not believe that they can dispute with you before your Lord—rather, you are the ones who dispute with others. God answers them: “Say: ‘Guidance is God’s guidance,’” and “[Say:] ‘Bounty is in God’s hand.’”<\/p><p>Alternatively, “that anyone is given” is an expression of purpose or cause (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مفعول لأجله<\/bdi>), governed by an omitted expression (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عامل محذوف<\/bdi>). The reconstructed sense is: did you devise what you devised because you disliked anyone being given what you were given, and feared that they might dispute with you before your Lord? <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N036\">[XIV-N036]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “that anyone is given” ( أن يؤتى ) may be the object ( مفعول ) of “believe” ( تؤمنوا ), while “Say: ‘Guidance is God’s guidance’” is a parenthesis ( اعتراض ). The lām in li-man ( لمن ) is an additional linking particle ( صلة ), and “or that they might dispute with you” is coordinated with yuʾtā. The reconstructed sense ( التقدير ) is: do not believe that anyone is given the like of what you were given, except someone of your own religion; and do not believe that they can dispute with you before your Lord—rather, you are the ones who dispute with others. God answers them: “Say: ‘Guidance is God’s guidance,’” and “[Say:] ‘Bounty is in God’s hand.’” Alternatively, “that anyone is given” is an expression of purpose or cause ( مفعول لأجله ), governed by an omitted expression ( عامل محذوف ). The reconstructed sense is: did you devise what you devised because you disliked anyone being given what you were given, and feared that they might dispute with you before your Lord? [XIV-N036]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Three readings of the argument and its speakers","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, relates the Jews’ words: “And” they said, “do not believe”—do not acknowledge or credit—“that anyone is given the like of what you were given,” of knowledge, wisdom, the parting of the sea and other favours, “except one who follows” Judaism and belongs to “your religion.” Do not believe “that they might dispute with you before your Lord,” for your religion is sounder than theirs. The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Say” to them, “Guidance is God’s guidance”—by it He guides whom He wills—and “Bounty is in God’s hand; He gives it to whom He wills.”<\/p><p>Alternatively, the Real, exalted is His majesty, says: they said, do not believe or submit “except to one who follows your religion” and is of your own people, for prophethood belongs exclusively to you. The Real refutes them: “Say: ‘Guidance is God’s guidance.’” He assigns it to whichever of His servants He wills. How, then, can you confine it to yourselves? Because “anyone might be given the like of what you were given,” you said what you said and contrived what you contrived, out of envy and transgression; “or” because you feared that “they might dispute with you before your Lord,” overcoming you in argument through the manifest ascendancy of their religion. “Say,” Muḥammad: “Bounty is in God’s hand; He gives it to whom He wills.” No device or deception can turn it back.<\/p><p>Alternatively, the Real, exalted is His majesty, addresses the believers to make them steadfast and strengthen their hearts: do not believe, company of believers, that anyone is given the like of the bounty and upright religion you have been given, except someone who follows your true religion and brings it from the Real. Do not believe that they can dispute with you about your religion “before your Lord,” or that anyone is capable of this. Guidance is God’s guidance and bounty is in God’s hand: “He gives it to whom He wills. God is all-encompassing” in bounty and generosity, “knowing” who merits special distinction and bounty. “He singles out for His mercy whom He wills,” as with prophethood and other gifts. “God is the possessor of immense bounty”: His bounty has no limit, just as His Essence has no limit.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, relates the Jews’ words: “And” they said, “do not believe”—do not acknowledge or credit—“that anyone is given the like of what you were given,” of knowledge, wisdom, the parting of the sea and other favours, “except one who follows” Judaism and belongs to “your religion.” Do not believe “that they might dispute with you before your Lord,” for your religion is sounder than theirs. The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Say” to them, “Guidance is God’s guidance”—by it He guides whom He wills—and “Bounty is in God’s hand; He gives it to whom He wills.” Alternatively, the Real, exalted is His majesty, says: they said, do not believe or submit “except to one who follows your religion” and is of your own people, for prophethood belongs exclusively to you. The Real refutes them: “Say: ‘Guidance is God’s guidance.’” He assigns it to whichever of His servants He wills. How, then, can you confine it to yourselves? Because “anyone might be given the like of what you were given,” you said what you said and contrived what you contrived, out of envy and transgression; “or” because you feared that “they might dispute with you before your Lord,” overcoming you in argument through the manifest ascendancy of their religion. “Say,” Muḥammad: “Bounty is in God’s hand; He gives it to whom He wills.” No device or deception can turn it back. Alternatively, the Real, exalted is His majesty, addresses the believers to make them steadfast and strengthen their hearts: do not believe, company of believers, that anyone is given the like of the bounty and upright religion you have been given, except someone who follows your true religion and brings it from the Real. Do not believe that they can dispute with you about your religion “before your Lord,” or that anyone is capable of this. Guidance is God’s guidance and bounty is in God’s hand: “He gives it to whom He wills. God is all-encompassing” in bounty and generosity, “knowing” who merits special distinction and bounty. “He singles out for His mercy whom He wills,” as with prophethood and other gifts. “God is the possessor of immense bounty”: His bounty has no limit, just as His Essence has no limit."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Preparation, confidentiality and the danger of disclosure without realization","html":"<p>The Real—majestic is His Essence and mighty His power—says to the people of special distinction (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل الخصوصية<\/bdi>): acknowledge that distinction only to someone who shares your religion and path, has adopted your manner, and has expended himself and his means in your companionship. This is for fear that someone might be given the like of the distinction you have received without being qualified for it, and take it merely as knowledge; he might then fall into heresy or transgression. Or they might argue against you by the religious Law (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الشريعة<\/bdi>) and shed your blood, as happened to al-Ḥallāj, God be pleased with him. Concerning this the poet says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Whoever has witnessed the Reality, let him guard it,<br/>or he will be slain by the spear;<br/>like the Ḥallāj of love, when there appeared to him<br/>the sun of Reality through drawing near. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N037\">[XIV-N037]<\/a><\/blockquote><p>Another says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">If they divulge the secret, their blood is made forfeit;<br/>thus is the blood of those who divulge it made forfeit.<\/blockquote><p>Say, knower of God, to one who seeks special distinction before fulfilling its conditions, or denies its presence in those who do fulfil them: guidance is God’s guidance; by it He guides whom He wills. Bounty is in God’s hand; He gives it to whom He wills. Mercy—which here is special distinction—is in God’s grasp; He singles out whom He wills for it. “God is the possessor of immense bounty.” Whoever desires special distinction should seek it at its source: those who know it. Let him expend himself and his means in their service until they acquaint him with it. Success comes through God.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The Real—majestic is His Essence and mighty His power—says to the people of special distinction ( أهل الخصوصية ): acknowledge that distinction only to someone who shares your religion and path, has adopted your manner, and has expended himself and his means in your companionship. This is for fear that someone might be given the like of the distinction you have received without being qualified for it, and take it merely as knowledge; he might then fall into heresy or transgression. Or they might argue against you by the religious Law ( الشريعة ) and shed your blood, as happened to al-Ḥallāj, God be pleased with him. Concerning this the poet says: Whoever has witnessed the Reality, let him guard it, or he will be slain by the spear; like the Ḥallāj of love, when there appeared to him the sun of Reality through drawing near. [XIV-N037] Another says: If they divulge the secret, their blood is made forfeit; thus is the blood of those who divulge it made forfeit. Say, knower of God, to one who seeks special distinction before fulfilling its conditions, or denies its presence in those who do fulfil them: guidance is God’s guidance; by it He guides whom He wills. Bounty is in God’s hand; He gives it to whom He wills. Mercy—which here is special distinction—is in God’s grasp; He singles out whom He wills for it. “God is the possessor of immense bounty.” Whoever desires special distinction should seek it at its source: those who know it. Let him expend himself and his means in their service until they acquaint him with it. Success comes through God."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real Most High then mentioned the description of the Jews as betraying trusts, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real Most High then mentioned the description of the Jews as betraying trusts, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C369.1","page":369,"ar":"البيتان: من قصيدة للشيخ محيي الدين بن عربي، في كتابه: الإسراء إلى المقام الأسرى، وفيه: ومن فهم الإشارة فليصنها.","en":"The two verses are from a poem by Shaykh Muḥyī l-Dīn ibn ʿArabī in his book al-Isrāʾ ilā l-maqām al-asrā. There the wording is: “Let whoever understands the allusion keep it concealed.”","anchor":"له شمس الحقيقة بالتداني"}],"english_characters":4848,"arabic_characters":3126},{"id":"q3-75-76","q":"3:75–76","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:75–76 · Trustworthiness, the rights of others and false claims of spiritual exemption","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 370–371","cairo":"I:370–371","source_page_first":370,"source_page_last":371,"ar":"وَمِنْ أَهْلِ الْكِتابِ مَنْ إِنْ تَأْمَنْهُ بِقِنْطارٍ يُؤَدِّهِ إِلَيْكَ وَمِنْهُمْ مَنْ إِنْ تَأْمَنْهُ بِدِينارٍ لا يُؤَدِّهِ إِلَيْكَ إِلاَّ ما دُمْتَ عَلَيْهِ قائِماً ذلِكَ بِأَنَّهُمْ قالُوا لَيْسَ عَلَيْنا فِي الْأُمِّيِّينَ سَبِيلٌ وَيَقُولُونَ عَلَى اللَّهِ الْكَذِبَ وَهُمْ يَعْلَمُونَ (75) بَلى مَنْ أَوْفى بِعَهْدِهِ وَاتَّقى فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ يُحِبُّ الْمُتَّقِينَ (76)\nقلت: الباء في (بقنطار) ، بمعنى على، و (يؤده) : جواب الشرط مجزوم بحذف الياء، ومن قرأ بإسكان الضمير فلأنه أقامه مقام المحذوف، فجزمه عوضاً عنه، وقال الفراء: مذهب بعض العرب: يسكنون الهاء إذا تحرك ما قبلها، يقولون: ضربته ضرباً شديداً.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَمِنْ أَهْلِ الْكِتابِ من أسلم وآمن فصار من أهل الإيمان، إِنْ تَأْمَنْهُ على قنطار من المال أو أكثر أداه إليك، ولم يخن منه شيئاً. وفي الحديث: «من ائتمن على أمانة فأداها، ولو شاء لم يؤدها، زوجه الله من الحور العين ما شاء» . وَمِنْهُمْ من بقي على دينه من أهل الخيانة والخسران، إِنْ تَأْمَنْهُ على دينار فأقل لم يؤده إليك إِلَّا ما دُمْتَ عَلَيْهِ قائِماً على رأسه، مبالغاً في مطالبته. نزلت في عبد الله بن سلام، استودعه قرشي ألفاً ومائتي أوقية ذهباً، فأداها إليه، وفي فنحاص بن عازوراء اليهودي، استودعه قرشي آخر ديناراً، فجحده. وقيل: في النصارى واليهود، فإن النصارى: الغالب عليهم الأمانة، واليهود الغالب عليهم الخيانة.\nوذلك الاستحلال بسبب أنهم قالُوا لَيْسَ عَلَيْنا فِي الْأُمِّيِّينَ سَبِيلٌ أي: ليس علينا في شأن من ليسوا أهل كتاب، ولم يكونوا على ديننا، حرج في أخذ مالهم وجحدها، ولا إثم، وَيَقُولُونَ عَلَى اللَّهِ الْكَذِبَ وَهُمْ يَعْلَمُونَ أنهم كاذبون لأنهم استحلوا ظلم من خالفهم، وقالوا: لم يجعل لهم في التوراة حرمة.\nوقيل: عامل اليهود رجالا من قريش، فلما أسلموا تقاضوهم، فقالوا: سقط حقكم حيث تركتم دينكم. وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم:\n«كَذَبَ أَعْدَاءُ اللهِ، مَا مِنْ شَيءٍ فِي الجاهِلية إلاّ وَهُو تَحْتَ قَدَمَي، إِلاَّ الأمَانَةَ فإِنهَا مُؤداةٌ إلى الْبَر والفَاجِر» .\nثم كذَّبهم الحقّ- تعالى- فقال: بَلى عليهم في ذلك سبيل، فإن مَنْ أَوْفى بِعَهْدِهِ وَاتَّقى الشرك والمعاصي فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ يُحِبُّ الْمُتَّقِينَ ومن أحبّه الله كيف يباح ماله وتسقط حرمته؟! بل من أسقط حرمته فقد حارب الله ورسوله، أو مَنْ أَوْفى، بعهد الله من أهل الكتاب، فآمن بمحمد- عليه الصلاة والسلام- وَاتَّقى الخيانة، وأدى الأمانة، فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ يُحِبُّ الْمُتَّقِينَ. وأوقع المظهر موقع الضمير العائد إلى «من» لعمومه، فإن لفظ المتقين عام يصدق برد الودائع وغيره، إشعاراً بأن التقوى ملاك الأمر وسبب الحفظ. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: قد رأينا بعض الفقراء دخل بلد الحقيقة فسقطت من قلبه هيبة الشريعة، فتساهل في أموال الناس وسقطت لديه حرمة العباد، حتى لا تثق به في حفظ مال ولا أهل، فإذا أودعته شيئاً أو قارضته لا يرده إليك إلا ما دمت عليه قائماً. وهذه زندقة ونزعة إسرائيلية، لا يرضاها أدنى الناس، فما بالك بمن يدعي أنه أعلى الناس، وفي بعض الحكم: [كَمَالُ الديانة ترك الخيانة] ، وأعظم الإفلاس خيانة الناس، وفي الحديث: «ثَلاَثٌ مَنْ كُنَّ فيه فهُوَ مُنَافقٌ، وإن صَلَّى وإن صَامَ وزَعم أنه مؤمن، إذا حدّث كذب، وإذا وعد أخلف، وإذا ائتمن خَانَ» . فإذا احتج لنفسه الأمارة، وقال: لا سبيل علينا في متاع العوام، فقد خلع من عنقه ربقة الإسلام، واستحق أن يعلو مفرقه الحُسام. والله تعالى أعلم.\nومن جملة الخيانة: أكل أموال الناس بالأيمان الفاجرة، كما أشار إلى ذلك الحق- تعالى- فقال:","quran_ar":"وَمِنْ أَهْلِ الْكِتابِ مَنْ إِنْ تَأْمَنْهُ بِقِنْطارٍ يُؤَدِّهِ إِلَيْكَ وَمِنْهُمْ مَنْ إِنْ تَأْمَنْهُ بِدِينارٍ لا يُؤَدِّهِ إِلَيْكَ إِلاَّ ما دُمْتَ عَلَيْهِ قائِماً ذلِكَ بِأَنَّهُمْ قالُوا لَيْسَ عَلَيْنا فِي الْأُمِّيِّينَ سَبِيلٌ وَيَقُولُونَ عَلَى اللَّهِ الْكَذِبَ وَهُمْ يَعْلَمُونَ (75) بَلى مَنْ أَوْفى بِعَهْدِهِ وَاتَّقى فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ يُحِبُّ الْمُتَّقِينَ (76)","quran_en":"75. Among the People of the Book is one who, if you entrust him with a great sum, will return it to you; and among them is one who, if you entrust him with a single dinar, will not return it unless you keep standing over him. That is because they say: “We are not answerable concerning the unlettered.” They knowingly speak falsehood against God. 76. Rather, whoever fulfils his covenant and is mindful of God—God loves the God-fearing.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The preposition of entrusting and the jussive form yuʾaddihi","html":"<p>I say: the bāʾ in bi-qinṭārin (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بقنطار<\/bdi>) has the meaning of ʿalā. “He will return it” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">يؤدّه<\/bdi>) is the response to the condition (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">جواب الشرط<\/bdi>), made jussive (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مجزوم<\/bdi>) by deletion of the yāʾ. In the reading that leaves the pronoun unvowelled, the pronoun is put in the place of the deleted letter and made quiescent in compensation for it. Al-Farrāʾ says that some Arabs leave the hāʾ unvowelled when the preceding letter is vowelled; they say, for example, ḍarabtuh ḍarban shadīdan, “I struck him a severe blow.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: the bāʾ in bi-qinṭārin ( بقنطار ) has the meaning of ʿalā. “He will return it” ( يؤدّه ) is the response to the condition ( جواب الشرط ), made jussive ( مجزوم ) by deletion of the yāʾ. In the reading that leaves the pronoun unvowelled, the pronoun is put in the place of the deleted letter and made quiescent in compensation for it. Al-Farrāʾ says that some Arabs leave the hāʾ unvowelled when the preceding letter is vowelled; they say, for example, ḍarabtuh ḍarban shadīdan, “I struck him a severe blow.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"An entrusted deposit remains another person’s right","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Among the People of the Book” are those who have submitted and believed, becoming people of faith. If you entrust such a person with “a great sum” of money, or more, he returns it to you without betraying any part of it. A hadith states: “Whoever is entrusted with a trust and returns it, although he could have withheld it, God will marry him to as many of the wide-eyed maidens as he wishes.” Among them are also those who remain in their religion, people of treachery and loss: entrust such a person with “a single dinar,” or less, and “he will not return it to you unless you keep standing over him,” insistently demanding it. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N038\">[XIV-N038]<\/a><\/p><p>The verse was revealed concerning ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām: a Qurashī deposited twelve hundred ūqiyyas of gold with him, which he returned. It also concerned Finḥāṣ ibn ʿĀzūrāʾ, the Jew: another Qurashī deposited a dinar with him, which he denied. Another explanation takes the verse to concern Christians and Jews, saying that trustworthiness predominates among the Christians and treachery among the Jews.<\/p><p>Their treating this as lawful arose because “they say: ‘We are not answerable concerning the unlettered.’” That is: with respect to people who are not People of the Book and do not share our religion, there is no blame or sin upon us in taking their property or denying it. “They knowingly speak falsehood against God”: they know they are lying, since they make it lawful to wrong those who differ from them and say that the Torah grants such people no inviolability.<\/p><p>Another account says that Jews had dealings with some men of Quraysh. When those men became Muslim and demanded payment, they replied: “Your right has lapsed because you abandoned your religion.” The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, said: “God’s enemies have lied. Everything belonging to the Age of Ignorance is beneath my feet, except a trust: it must be returned to the righteous and the unrighteous alike.”<\/p><p>The Real Most High then declares them false: “Rather,” they are indeed answerable in this matter. “Whoever fulfils his covenant and is mindful of God,” avoiding association of partners and acts of disobedience—“God loves the God-fearing.” How could the property of someone God loves be made lawful to take, or his inviolability lapse? Rather, whoever abolishes his inviolability has made war upon God and His Messenger. Alternatively: whoever among the People of the Book fulfils God’s covenant, believes in Muḥammad, blessings and peace be upon him, avoids treachery and returns the trust—“God loves the God-fearing.”<\/p><p>He puts the explicit noun (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المظهر<\/bdi>) in the place of a pronoun referring back to “whoever” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مَن<\/bdi>), to make the statement general. “The God-fearing” is a general expression applying to returning deposits and to other things, indicating that God-fearingness (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التقوى<\/bdi>) is the governing principle of the matter and the cause of preservation. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Among the People of the Book” are those who have submitted and believed, becoming people of faith. If you entrust such a person with “a great sum” of money, or more, he returns it to you without betraying any part of it. A hadith states: “Whoever is entrusted with a trust and returns it, although he could have withheld it, God will marry him to as many of the wide-eyed maidens as he wishes.” Among them are also those who remain in their religion, people of treachery and loss: entrust such a person with “a single dinar,” or less, and “he will not return it to you unless you keep standing over him,” insistently demanding it. [XIV-N038] The verse was revealed concerning ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām: a Qurashī deposited twelve hundred ūqiyyas of gold with him, which he returned. It also concerned Finḥāṣ ibn ʿĀzūrāʾ, the Jew: another Qurashī deposited a dinar with him, which he denied. Another explanation takes the verse to concern Christians and Jews, saying that trustworthiness predominates among the Christians and treachery among the Jews. Their treating this as lawful arose because “they say: ‘We are not answerable concerning the unlettered.’” That is: with respect to people who are not People of the Book and do not share our religion, there is no blame or sin upon us in taking their property or denying it. “They knowingly speak falsehood against God”: they know they are lying, since they make it lawful to wrong those who differ from them and say that the Torah grants such people no inviolability. Another account says that Jews had dealings with some men of Quraysh. When those men became Muslim and demanded payment, they replied: “Your right has lapsed because you abandoned your religion.” The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, said: “God’s enemies have lied. Everything belonging to the Age of Ignorance is beneath my feet, except a trust: it must be returned to the righteous and the unrighteous alike.” The Real Most High then declares them false: “Rather,” they are indeed answerable in this matter. “Whoever fulfils his covenant and is mindful of God,” avoiding association of partners and acts of disobedience—“God loves the God-fearing.” How could the property of someone God loves be made lawful to take, or his inviolability lapse? Rather, whoever abolishes his inviolability has made war upon God and His Messenger. Alternatively: whoever among the People of the Book fulfils God’s covenant, believes in Muḥammad, blessings and peace be upon him, avoids treachery and returns the trust—“God loves the God-fearing.” He puts the explicit noun ( المظهر ) in the place of a pronoun referring back to “whoever” ( مَن ), to make the statement general. “The God-fearing” is a general expression applying to returning deposits and to other things, indicating that God-fearingness ( التقوى ) is the governing principle of the matter and the cause of preservation. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Realization does not cancel the Law or people’s rights","html":"<p>We have seen some of the spiritually poor enter the land of Reality (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحقيقة<\/bdi>), whereupon reverence for the religious Law (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الشريعة<\/bdi>) falls away from their hearts. They become careless with people’s property, and the inviolability of God’s servants loses its hold upon them, until you cannot trust them to protect either property or family. Deposit something with such a person, or entrust him with capital in a profit-sharing investment (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قِراض<\/bdi>), and he will not return it unless you keep standing over him. This is heresy (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">زندقة<\/bdi>), an Israelite tendency that even the lowest of people would not accept—how much less someone claiming to be the highest! <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N039\">[XIV-N039]<\/a><\/p><p>Among the sayings of wisdom is: “The perfection of religious integrity is to abandon treachery.” The greatest bankruptcy is betraying people. A hadith states: “Three qualities make a person a hypocrite, even if he prays, fasts and claims to be a believer: when he speaks he lies, when he promises he breaks his promise, and when entrusted he betrays the trust.” If he argues on behalf of his soul that commands evil (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">النفس الأمّارة<\/bdi>), saying, “We are not answerable concerning the possessions of ordinary people,” he has cast the bond of Islam from his neck and deserves the sharp sword to fall upon the crown of his head. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"We have seen some of the spiritually poor enter the land of Reality ( الحقيقة ), whereupon reverence for the religious Law ( الشريعة ) falls away from their hearts. They become careless with people’s property, and the inviolability of God’s servants loses its hold upon them, until you cannot trust them to protect either property or family. Deposit something with such a person, or entrust him with capital in a profit-sharing investment ( قِراض ), and he will not return it unless you keep standing over him. This is heresy ( زندقة ), an Israelite tendency that even the lowest of people would not accept—how much less someone claiming to be the highest! [XIV-N039] Among the sayings of wisdom is: “The perfection of religious integrity is to abandon treachery.” The greatest bankruptcy is betraying people. A hadith states: “Three qualities make a person a hypocrite, even if he prays, fasts and claims to be a believer: when he speaks he lies, when he promises he breaks his promise, and when entrusted he betrays the trust.” If he argues on behalf of his soul that commands evil ( النفس الأمّارة ), saying, “We are not answerable concerning the possessions of ordinary people,” he has cast the bond of Islam from his neck and deserves the sharp sword to fall upon the crown of his head. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>One form of treachery is consuming people’s property through wicked oaths, as the Real Most High indicates, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"One form of treachery is consuming people’s property through wicked oaths, as the Real Most High indicates, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":4912,"arabic_characters":3071},{"id":"q3-77","q":"3:77","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:77 · Selling the covenant; worldly inducement and the covenant of spirits","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 371–372","cairo":"I:371–372","source_page_first":371,"source_page_last":372,"ar":"إِنَّ الَّذِينَ يَشْتَرُونَ بِعَهْدِ اللَّهِ وَأَيْمانِهِمْ ثَمَناً قَلِيلاً أُولئِكَ لا خَلاقَ لَهُمْ فِي الْآخِرَةِ وَلا يُكَلِّمُهُمُ اللَّهُ وَلا يَنْظُرُ إِلَيْهِمْ يَوْمَ الْقِيامَةِ وَلا يُزَكِّيهِمْ وَلَهُمْ عَذابٌ أَلِيمٌ (77)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: إِنَّ الَّذِينَ يَشْتَرُونَ بِعَهْدِ اللَّهِ أي: يستبدلون بالوفاء بعهد الله كالإيمان بالرسول- عليه الصلاة والسلام- الذي أخذ على بني إسرائيل في التوراة وبيان صفته، وأداء الأمانة، فكتموا ذلك واستبدلوا به ثَمَناً قَلِيلًا حطاماً فانياً من الدنيا، كانوا يأخذونه من سفلتهم، فخافوا إن بيَّنوا ذلك زال ذلك عنهم، وكذلك الأيمان التي أخذها الله عليهم لئن أدركوا محمدا صلّى الله عليه وسلم ليؤمنن به ولينصرنه، فنقضوها، خوفاً من زوال رئاستهم، فاستبدلوا بالوفاء بها ثمناً قليلا فانياً، أُولئِكَ لا خَلاقَ لَهُمْ أي: لا نصيب لهم، فِي الْآخِرَةِ، وَلا يُكَلِّمُهُمُ اللَّهُ بما يسرهم، أو بشيء أصلاً، وإنما الملائكة تسألهم، وَلا يَنْظُرُ إِلَيْهِمْ يَوْمَ الْقِيامَةِ نظرة رحمة، بل يعرض عنهم، غضباً عليهم وهواناً بهم، وَلا يُزَكِّيهِمْ لا يطهرهم من ذنوبهم، أو لا يُثني عليهم، وَلَهُمْ عَذابٌ أَلِيمٌ أي: موجع.\nقال عكرمة: نزلت في أبي رافع وكنانة بن أَبي الحقيق وحُيي بن أخطب، وغيرهم من رؤساء اليهود، كتموا ما عهد الله إليهم في التوراة في شأن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم من بيان صفته، فكتموا ذلك وكتبوا غيره، وحلفوا أنه من عند الله، لئلا يفوتهم الرشا من أتباعهم.\nوقال الكلبي: إن ناساً من علماء اليهود كانوا ذا حظ من علم التوراة، فأصابتهم سنة، فأتوا كعب بن الأشرف يستميرونه، أي: يطلبون منه الميرة- وهو الطعام-، فقال لهم كعب: هل تعلمون أن هذا الرجل رسول في كتابكم؟\nقالوا: نعم، أو ما تعلمه أنت؟ قال: لا، قالوا: فإنا نشهد أنه عبد الله ورسوله، قال كعب: لقد قدمتم عليَّ، وأنا أريد أن أميركم وأكسوكم، فَحَرَمَكُم الله خيراً كثيراً، قالوا: فإنه شُبه لنا، فرُوَيْداً حتى نلقاه، فانطلقوا، فكتبوا صفة غير صفته، ثم أتوا نبى الله- عليه الصلاة والسلام- فكلموه، ثم رجعوا إلى كعب، فقالوا: قد كنا نرى أنه رسول الله، فأتيناه فإذا هو ليس بالنعت الذي نُعت لنا، وأخرجوا الذي كتبوه، ففرح كعب، ومارهم. فنزلت الآية. قلت: انظر الطمع، وما يصنع بصاحبه! والعياذ بالله.\nوقيل: نزلت في رجل أقام سلعته في السوق، وحلف لقد أعطى فيها كذا وكذا، وقيل: نزلت في الأشعث بن قيس، كانت بينه وبين رجل خصومة، فتوجهت اليمين على الرجل، فأراد أن يحلف. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: قد أخذ الله العهد على الأرواح ألا يعبدوا معه غيره، ولا يميلوا إلى شيء سواه، فكل من مال إلى شيء أو ركن بالمحبة إلى غير الله، فقد نقض العهد مع الله، فلا نصيب له في مقام المعرفة، ولا تحصل له مشاهدة ولا مكالمة حتى يثوب ويتوجه بكليته إلى مولاه. والله- تعالى- أعلم.\nومن مساوئهم أيضا: تحريفهم لكتاب الله، كما أشار إلى ذلك الحق- تعالى- بقوله:","quran_ar":"إِنَّ الَّذِينَ يَشْتَرُونَ بِعَهْدِ اللَّهِ وَأَيْمانِهِمْ ثَمَناً قَلِيلاً أُولئِكَ لا خَلاقَ لَهُمْ فِي الْآخِرَةِ وَلا يُكَلِّمُهُمُ اللَّهُ وَلا يَنْظُرُ إِلَيْهِمْ يَوْمَ الْقِيامَةِ وَلا يُزَكِّيهِمْ وَلَهُمْ عَذابٌ أَلِيمٌ (77)","quran_en":"77. Those who exchange God’s covenant and their oaths for a paltry price have no share in the Hereafter. God will not speak to them or look upon them on the Day of Resurrection, nor will He purify them. Theirs will be a painful punishment.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The paltry price of concealing a binding trust","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Those who exchange God’s covenant”—who barter away fidelity to it, such as belief in the Messenger, blessings and peace be upon him, the covenant taken from the Children of Israel in the Torah, disclosure of his description, and the discharge of trusts. They concealed this and exchanged it for “a paltry price”: the perishing debris of this world that they received from their common followers. They feared that disclosing it would cost them these receipts. Likewise, they broke the oaths God had taken from them that, should they live to encounter Muḥammad, they would believe in him and support him, for fear of losing their leadership. In place of fidelity, they took a paltry, perishing price.<\/p><p>“They have no share”—no portion—“in the Hereafter. God will not speak to them” with anything that pleases them, or will not speak to them at all: only the angels question them. He will not “look upon them on the Day of Resurrection” with a look of mercy, but will turn from them in anger and make them abased. He will not “purify them” of their sins, or praise them. “Theirs will be a painful,” grievous, “punishment.”<\/p><p>ʿIkrima said: it was revealed concerning Abū Rāfiʿ, Kināna ibn Abī al-Ḥuqayq, Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab and other Jewish leaders. They concealed what God had entrusted to them in the Torah concerning disclosure of the Prophet’s description, wrote something else, and swore that it came from God, so that they would not lose their followers’ bribes.<\/p><p>Al-Kalbī said: some Jewish scholars with a share of knowledge of the Torah were afflicted by a year of famine. They went to Kaʿb ibn al-Ashraf to seek provisions—that is, food. Kaʿb asked them: “Do you know that this man is a messenger in your scripture?” They answered: “Yes. Do you not know it yourself?” He said: “No.” They replied: “We testify that he is God’s servant and Messenger.” Kaʿb said: “You came to me when I intended to provision and clothe you; God has now deprived you of much good.” They said: “We were confused about him. Give us a little time to meet him.” They went away and wrote a description different from his. Then they went to God’s Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, and spoke to him. Returning to Kaʿb, they said: “We used to think he was God’s Messenger, but when we visited him, he did not match the description given to us.” They produced what they had written, and Kaʿb rejoiced and supplied them with provisions. The verse was then revealed. I say: look at avarice and what it does to its possessor! In God we seek refuge.<\/p><p>Another account says it was revealed concerning a man who displayed his merchandise in the market and swore that he had been offered such-and-such a price for it. Another says it concerned al-Ashʿath ibn Qays, who had a dispute with a man upon whom the oath then devolved; the man intended to swear it. And God Most High knows best. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N040\">[XIV-N040]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Those who exchange God’s covenant”—who barter away fidelity to it, such as belief in the Messenger, blessings and peace be upon him, the covenant taken from the Children of Israel in the Torah, disclosure of his description, and the discharge of trusts. They concealed this and exchanged it for “a paltry price”: the perishing debris of this world that they received from their common followers. They feared that disclosing it would cost them these receipts. Likewise, they broke the oaths God had taken from them that, should they live to encounter Muḥammad, they would believe in him and support him, for fear of losing their leadership. In place of fidelity, they took a paltry, perishing price. “They have no share”—no portion—“in the Hereafter. God will not speak to them” with anything that pleases them, or will not speak to them at all: only the angels question them. He will not “look upon them on the Day of Resurrection” with a look of mercy, but will turn from them in anger and make them abased. He will not “purify them” of their sins, or praise them. “Theirs will be a painful,” grievous, “punishment.” ʿIkrima said: it was revealed concerning Abū Rāfiʿ, Kināna ibn Abī al-Ḥuqayq, Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab and other Jewish leaders. They concealed what God had entrusted to them in the Torah concerning disclosure of the Prophet’s description, wrote something else, and swore that it came from God, so that they would not lose their followers’ bribes. Al-Kalbī said: some Jewish scholars with a share of knowledge of the Torah were afflicted by a year of famine. They went to Kaʿb ibn al-Ashraf to seek provisions—that is, food. Kaʿb asked them: “Do you know that this man is a messenger in your scripture?” They answered: “Yes. Do you not know it yourself?” He said: “No.” They replied: “We testify that he is God’s servant and Messenger.” Kaʿb said: “You came to me when I intended to provision and clothe you; God has now deprived you of much good.” They said: “We were confused about him. Give us a little time to meet him.” They went away and wrote a description different from his. Then they went to God’s Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, and spoke to him. Returning to Kaʿb, they said: “We used to think he was God’s Messenger, but when we visited him, he did not match the description given to us.” They produced what they had written, and Kaʿb rejoiced and supplied them with provisions. The verse was then revealed. I say: look at avarice and what it does to its possessor! In God we seek refuge. Another account says it was revealed concerning a man who displayed his merchandise in the market and swore that he had been offered such-and-such a price for it. Another says it concerned al-Ashʿath ibn Qays, who had a dispute with a man upon whom the oath then devolved; the man intended to swear it. And God Most High knows best. [XIV-N040]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The covenant that no other should share the heart’s devotion","html":"<p>God took the covenant from the spirits (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأرواح<\/bdi>) that they should worship none with Him and incline to nothing other than Him. Whoever inclines to a thing, or rests in love upon someone other than God, has broken his covenant with God. He has no portion in the station of knowledge of God (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المعرفة<\/bdi>), nor does witnessing (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المشاهدة<\/bdi>) or divine converse (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المكالمة<\/bdi>) come to him until he returns and turns in his entirety towards his Master. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"God took the covenant from the spirits ( الأرواح ) that they should worship none with Him and incline to nothing other than Him. Whoever inclines to a thing, or rests in love upon someone other than God, has broken his covenant with God. He has no portion in the station of knowledge of God ( المعرفة ), nor does witnessing ( المشاهدة ) or divine converse ( المكالمة ) come to him until he returns and turns in his entirety towards his Master. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Another of their faults is altering God’s scripture, as the Real Most High indicates in His words:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Another of their faults is altering God’s scripture, as the Real Most High indicates in His words:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3478,"arabic_characters":2500},{"id":"q3-78","q":"3:78","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:78 · Distorting scripture, corrupt judgments and the imitation of spiritual knowledge","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 372–373","cairo":"I:372–373","source_page_first":372,"source_page_last":373,"ar":"وَإِنَّ مِنْهُمْ لَفَرِيقاً يَلْوُونَ أَلْسِنَتَهُمْ بِالْكِتابِ لِتَحْسَبُوهُ مِنَ الْكِتابِ وَما هُوَ مِنَ الْكِتابِ وَيَقُولُونَ هُوَ مِنْ عِنْدِ اللَّهِ وَما هُوَ مِنْ عِنْدِ اللَّهِ وَيَقُولُونَ عَلَى اللَّهِ الْكَذِبَ وَهُمْ يَعْلَمُونَ (78)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَإِنَّ من أهل الكتاب لَفَرِيقاً، وهو كعب بن الأشرف، وحيي بن أخطب، ومالك بن الصيف، وأبو ياسر، وشعبة بن عامر، يَلْوُونَ أي: يفتلون أَلْسِنَتَهُمْ بِالْكِتابِ أي: التوراة عند قراءته، فيميلون عن المنزل إلى المُحرف، لِتَحْسَبُوهُ مِنَ الْكِتابِ أي: لتظنوا أن ذلك المحرف من التوراة، وَما هُوَ مِنَ الْكِتابِ وَيَقُولُونَ هُوَ مِنْ عِنْدِ اللَّهِ وَما هُوَ مِنْ عِنْدِ اللَّهِ وَيَقُولُونَ عَلَى اللَّهِ الْكَذِبَ فيما نسبوا إليه، وَهُمْ يَعْلَمُونَ أنه ليس من عند الله.\nقال ابن عباس: نزلت في اليهود والنصارى جميعاً، حرفوا التوراة والإنجيل، وألحقوا به ما ليس منه، وأسقطوا منه الدين الحنيف، فبيَّن الله كذبهم. وقيل: في الرجم، حيث كتموا الرجم، وألقى قارئ التوراة يده على آية الرجم، وقرأ ما حولها، فقال له ابنُ سلام: ارفع يديك، فإذا آية الرجم تلوح. والله أعلم.\nالإشارة: هذه الآية تنسحب على علماء السوء، الذين يفتون بغير المشهور، لحظ يأخذونه من الدنيا، وعلى قضاة الجور الذين يحكمون بالهوى، ويعتمدون على الأقوال الواهية، ويقولون هو من عند الله، وما هو من عند الله.\nوكذلك بعض المنتسبين من الفقراء، يتصنعون إلى العامة، يطمعون فيما في أيديهم من الحطام، فيظهرون لهم علوماً ومعارف وحكماً، يلوون ألسنتهم بها وقلوبهم خاوية من معناها، فظاهر حالهم يُوهم أن ذلك موافق لقلوبهم، وأنهم عاملون بذلك، وباطنهم يكذبهم في ذلك، (وَاللَّهُ يهدي مَن يشاء إلى صراط مستقيم) .\nثم أبطل الله تعالى شبهة اليهود والنصارى فى عبادة عيسى وعزير وغيرهم، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَإِنَّ مِنْهُمْ لَفَرِيقاً يَلْوُونَ أَلْسِنَتَهُمْ بِالْكِتابِ لِتَحْسَبُوهُ مِنَ الْكِتابِ وَما هُوَ مِنَ الْكِتابِ وَيَقُولُونَ هُوَ مِنْ عِنْدِ اللَّهِ وَما هُوَ مِنْ عِنْدِ اللَّهِ وَيَقُولُونَ عَلَى اللَّهِ الْكَذِبَ وَهُمْ يَعْلَمُونَ (78)","quran_en":"78. Among them is a group who twist their tongues with the scripture so that you might think it belongs to the scripture, though it does not belong to the scripture. They say, “It is from God,” though it is not from God. They knowingly speak falsehood against God.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Presenting altered words as revealed scripture","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Among” the People of the Book “is a group”—Kaʿb ibn al-Ashraf, Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab, Mālik ibn al-Ṣayf, Abū Yāsir and Shuʿba ibn ʿĀmir—“who twist,” that is, turn and contort, “their tongues with the scripture,” the Torah, when reciting it. They turn from what was revealed to what has been altered, “so that you might think it belongs to the scripture”: suppose that the altered material belongs to the Torah, “though it does not belong to the scripture.” They say, “‘It is from God,’ though it is not from God.” They “knowingly speak falsehood against God” in what they attribute to Him, knowing that it is not from Him.<\/p><p>Ibn ʿAbbās said: it was revealed concerning both Jews and Christians, who altered the Torah and the Gospel, added what did not belong to them, and removed the upright religion; God made their falsehood clear. Another account concerns stoning: they concealed it, and the reader of the Torah put his hand over the verse on stoning while reciting what surrounded it. Ibn Salām told him to lift his hands, whereupon the verse on stoning was plainly visible. And God knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Among” the People of the Book “is a group”—Kaʿb ibn al-Ashraf, Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab, Mālik ibn al-Ṣayf, Abū Yāsir and Shuʿba ibn ʿĀmir—“who twist,” that is, turn and contort, “their tongues with the scripture,” the Torah, when reciting it. They turn from what was revealed to what has been altered, “so that you might think it belongs to the scripture”: suppose that the altered material belongs to the Torah, “though it does not belong to the scripture.” They say, “‘It is from God,’ though it is not from God.” They “knowingly speak falsehood against God” in what they attribute to Him, knowing that it is not from Him. Ibn ʿAbbās said: it was revealed concerning both Jews and Christians, who altered the Torah and the Gospel, added what did not belong to them, and removed the upright religion; God made their falsehood clear. Another account concerns stoning: they concealed it, and the reader of the Torah put his hand over the verse on stoning while reciting what surrounded it. Ibn Salām told him to lift his hands, whereupon the verse on stoning was plainly visible. And God knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Worldly gain behind scholarly and spiritual appearances","html":"<p>This verse extends to corrupt scholars who issue rulings contrary to the established predominant position (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المشهور<\/bdi>) for a worldly portion they receive, and to unjust judges who judge by desire and rely on unsound opinions, saying, “It is from God,” although it is not from God. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N041\">[XIV-N041]<\/a><\/p><p>So too with certain people affiliated with the spiritually poor: they put on a performance for the public, coveting the worldly debris in their hands. They display sciences, spiritual knowledge and words of wisdom, twisting their tongues around them, while their hearts are empty of their meaning. Their outward appearance suggests that these things correspond to their hearts and that they act upon them, but their inward state gives them the lie. “God guides whom He wills to a straight path.”<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"This verse extends to corrupt scholars who issue rulings contrary to the established predominant position ( المشهور ) for a worldly portion they receive, and to unjust judges who judge by desire and rely on unsound opinions, saying, “It is from God,” although it is not from God. [XIV-N041] So too with certain people affiliated with the spiritually poor: they put on a performance for the public, coveting the worldly debris in their hands. They display sciences, spiritual knowledge and words of wisdom, twisting their tongues around them, while their hearts are empty of their meaning. Their outward appearance suggests that these things correspond to their hearts and that they act upon them, but their inward state gives them the lie. “God guides whom He wills to a straight path.”"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>God Most High then refuted the misconception of the Jews and Christians concerning the worship of Jesus, Ezra and others, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"God Most High then refuted the misconception of the Jews and Christians concerning the worship of Jesus, Ezra and others, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2048,"arabic_characters":1584},{"id":"q3-79-80","q":"3:79–80","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:79–80 · God-centred teachers; veneration, spiritual training and the limits of mediation","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 373–374","cairo":"I:373–374","source_page_first":373,"source_page_last":374,"ar":"ما كانَ لِبَشَرٍ أَنْ يُؤْتِيَهُ اللَّهُ الْكِتابَ وَالْحُكْمَ وَالنُّبُوَّةَ ثُمَّ يَقُولَ لِلنَّاسِ كُونُوا عِباداً لِي مِنْ دُونِ اللَّهِ وَلكِنْ كُونُوا رَبَّانِيِّينَ بِما كُنْتُمْ تُعَلِّمُونَ الْكِتابَ وَبِما كُنْتُمْ تَدْرُسُونَ (79) وَلا يَأْمُرُكُمْ أَنْ تَتَّخِذُوا الْمَلائِكَةَ وَالنَّبِيِّينَ أَرْباباً أَيَأْمُرُكُمْ بِالْكُفْرِ بَعْدَ إِذْ أَنْتُمْ مُسْلِمُونَ (80)\nقلت: البشر: اسم جمع لا مفرد له، يطلق على الجماعة والواحد. والرباني: هو الذي يُربي الناس ويؤدبهم ويُهذبهم بالعلم والعمل. وقال ابن عباس: (هو الذي يُربي الناس بصغار العلم قبل كباره) ، والنون فيه للمبالغة، كلحياني ورقباني. و (لا يأمركم) بالرفع، استئناف، وبالنصب: عطف على «يقول» ، و «لا» مزيدة،: أي ما كان لبشر أن يستنبئه الله، ثم يأمر بعبادة نفسه، ويأمر باتخاذ الملائكة أرباباً. أو غير مزيدة، والتقدير: ليس له أن يأمر بعبادته ولا باتخاذ الملائكة أرباباً.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: ما كانَ ينبغي لِبَشَرٍ أَنْ يُؤْتِيَهُ اللَّهُ الْكِتابَ وَالْحُكْمَ أي: الفصل بين العباد، وَالنُّبُوَّةَ أي: الوحي بالأحكام، ثُمَّ يَقُولَ بعد ذلك لِلنَّاسِ كُونُوا عِباداً لِي مِنْ دُونِ اللَّهِ أو مع الله، أو يرضى أن يعبد من دون الله، وَلكِنْ يقول لهم: كُونُوا رَبَّانِيِّينَ أي: علماء بالله، فقهاء في دينه، حلماء على الناس، تُربون الناس بالعلم والعمل والهمة والحال، بسبب بما كنتم تعلمون من كتاب الله وَبِما كُنْتُمْ تَدْرُسُونَ منه، أو بِما كُنْتُمْ تُعَلِّمُونَ الناس من الخير بكتاب الله، وما كنتم تدرسونه عليهم. ولمّا مات ابن عباس- رضى الله عنهما- قال محمد بن الحنفية: (مات ربَّاني هذه الأمة) .\nولا يأمركم ذلك البشر الذي خصه الله بالنبوة، أَنْ تَتَّخِذُوا الْمَلائِكَةَ وَالنَّبِيِّينَ أَرْباباً من دون الله، أَيَأْمُرُكُمْ بِالْكُفْرِ بَعْدَ إِذْ أَنْتُمْ مُسْلِمُونَ أي: منقادون لأحكام الله. قيل: سبب نزول الآية: أن نصارى نجران قالوا: يا محمد تريد أن نعبدك ونتخذك ربا؟ فقال النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم: «معاذ الله أن نعبد غيرَ الله، أو نَأمُرَ بعبادة غيره» . وقيل: أن رجلاً قال: يا رسول الله: نُسلِّم عليك كما يُسلِّم بعضُنا على بعض، أفلا نسجُد لك؟ فقال:\n«لا يَنْبَغِي ان يسجد احدٌ لأحدٍ من دُونِ اللهِ، ولكنْ أكْرِموا نَبِيَّكُمْ، واعْرِفُوا الحقَّ لأَهْلِه» .\nالإشارة: ما زال الفقراء يعظمون أشياخهم، ويبالغون في ذلك حتى يُقبِّلون أرجلهم والترابَ بين أيديهم، ويجتهدون في خدمتهم(1) ، فإذا رءاهم الأشياخ فعلوا ذلك سكتوا عنهم، لأن ذلك هو ربحهم وسبب فتحهم، وفي ذلك قال القائل:\nبذَبْح النفوس وحط الرءوس تصفى الكئوس\nلكنهم يرشدونهم إلى الحضرة، حتى يفنوهم عن شهود الواسطة، فيكون تعظيمهم وحط رأسهم إنما هو لله لا لغيره، وحينئذٍ يكونون ربانيين، علماء بالله مقربين، وكان شيخنا يقول: لا تزوروني على أني شيخكم، ولكن اعرفوا فينا، وافنوا عن رؤية حسنا، حتى يكون التعظيم إنما هو لله ربنا. هـ. فدلالة الأشياخ للفقراء على التعظيم والأدب ليس ذلك مقصوداً لأنفسهم، وحاشاهم من ذلك. ما كان لبشر أن يؤتيه الله الخصوصية ثم يقول للناس كونوا عباداً لي من دون الله، ولكن يقول لهم: كونوا ربانيين عارفين بالله، حتى يكون تعظيمكم إنما هو لله، ولا يأمر أيضاً بالفرق حتى يتخذوا الأشياء أرباباً من دون الله، ولكن يأمر بالجمع حتى يغيبوا عما سوى الله، وكيف يأمرهم بالفرق، وهو إنما يدلهم على الجمع؟ أيأمرهم بالكفر بعد أن كانوا مسلمين. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم ذكر أخذ الميثاق على الأنبياء وأممهم في الإيمان بالنبي صلّى الله عليه وسلم فقال:","quran_ar":"ما كانَ لِبَشَرٍ أَنْ يُؤْتِيَهُ اللَّهُ الْكِتابَ وَالْحُكْمَ وَالنُّبُوَّةَ ثُمَّ يَقُولَ لِلنَّاسِ كُونُوا عِباداً لِي مِنْ دُونِ اللَّهِ وَلكِنْ كُونُوا رَبَّانِيِّينَ بِما كُنْتُمْ تُعَلِّمُونَ الْكِتابَ وَبِما كُنْتُمْ تَدْرُسُونَ (79) وَلا يَأْمُرُكُمْ أَنْ تَتَّخِذُوا الْمَلائِكَةَ وَالنَّبِيِّينَ أَرْباباً أَيَأْمُرُكُمْ بِالْكُفْرِ بَعْدَ إِذْ أَنْتُمْ مُسْلِمُونَ (80)","quran_en":"79. It is not for a human being to whom God gives scripture, judgment and prophethood then to say to people, “Be servants of me apart from God.” Rather, [he says,] “Be God-centred teachers, through your teaching of the scripture and through your study of it.” 80. Nor does he command you to take angels and prophets as lords. Would he command you to disbelieve after you have submitted to God?","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Bashar, rabbānī and the readings of “he commands you”","html":"<p>I say: bashar (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">البشر<\/bdi>) is a collective expression (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اسم جمع<\/bdi>) with no singular form of its own; it is applied to a group and to an individual. A rabbānī is someone who nurtures people, trains their conduct and refines them through knowledge and action. Ibn ʿAbbās said: “He is one who nurtures people through the elementary matters of knowledge before its major ones.” The nūn in it conveys intensification, as in liḥyānī and raqabānī. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N042\">[XIV-N042]<\/a><\/p><p>With the indicative (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الرفع<\/bdi>), “nor does he command you” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ولا يأمركم<\/bdi>) begins a new statement (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">استئناف<\/bdi>). With the subjunctive (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">النصب<\/bdi>), it is coordinated with “say” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">يقول<\/bdi>), and lā is additional (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مزيدة<\/bdi>): it is not for a human being whom God makes a prophet then to command worship of himself and command the taking of angels as lords. Alternatively, lā is not additional, and the reconstructed sense is: he may neither command worship of himself nor the taking of angels as lords.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: bashar ( البشر ) is a collective expression ( اسم جمع ) with no singular form of its own; it is applied to a group and to an individual. A rabbānī is someone who nurtures people, trains their conduct and refines them through knowledge and action. Ibn ʿAbbās said: “He is one who nurtures people through the elementary matters of knowledge before its major ones.” The nūn in it conveys intensification, as in liḥyānī and raqabānī. [XIV-N042] With the indicative ( الرفع ), “nor does he command you” ( ولا يأمركم ) begins a new statement ( استئناف ). With the subjunctive ( النصب ), it is coordinated with “say” ( يقول ), and lā is additional ( مزيدة ): it is not for a human being whom God makes a prophet then to command worship of himself and command the taking of angels as lords. Alternatively, lā is not additional, and the reconstructed sense is: he may neither command worship of himself nor the taking of angels as lords."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Teaching that directs worship to God rather than the teacher","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: it would not be fitting for “a human being to whom God gives scripture, judgment”—adjudication between His servants—“and prophethood”—revelation of ordinances—“then to say to people: ‘Be servants of me apart from God,’” or alongside God, or to consent to being worshipped apart from God. Rather, he tells them: “Be God-centred teachers (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ربّانيين<\/bdi>)”: knowers of God, discerning in His religion and forbearing towards people, nurturing them through knowledge, action, spiritual aspiration (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الهمّة<\/bdi>) and spiritual state (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحال<\/bdi>), by virtue of what you know of God’s scripture and what you study of it; or by virtue of the good you teach people through God’s scripture and what you rehearse to them. When Ibn ʿAbbās, God be pleased with him and his father, died, Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥanafiyya said: “The rabbānī of this community has died.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N043\">[XIV-N043]<\/a><\/p><p>“Nor does he command you”—that human being whom God has specially favoured with prophethood—“to take angels and prophets as lords” apart from God. “Would he command you to disbelieve after you have submitted to God,” that is, yielded to His ordinances?<\/p><p>One account gives this occasion for the verse’s revelation: the Christians of Najrān said, “Muḥammad, do you want us to worship you and take you as a lord?” The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, answered: “God forbid that we worship anyone other than God, or command the worship of another.” Another says that a man asked: “Messenger of God, we greet you as we greet one another; should we not prostrate ourselves before you?” He answered: “No one should prostrate himself before anyone apart from God. Rather, honour your prophet and acknowledge the due of those to whom it belongs.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: it would not be fitting for “a human being to whom God gives scripture, judgment”—adjudication between His servants—“and prophethood”—revelation of ordinances—“then to say to people: ‘Be servants of me apart from God,’” or alongside God, or to consent to being worshipped apart from God. Rather, he tells them: “Be God-centred teachers ( ربّانيين )”: knowers of God, discerning in His religion and forbearing towards people, nurturing them through knowledge, action, spiritual aspiration ( الهمّة ) and spiritual state ( الحال ), by virtue of what you know of God’s scripture and what you study of it; or by virtue of the good you teach people through God’s scripture and what you rehearse to them. When Ibn ʿAbbās, God be pleased with him and his father, died, Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥanafiyya said: “The rabbānī of this community has died.” [XIV-N043] “Nor does he command you”—that human being whom God has specially favoured with prophethood—“to take angels and prophets as lords” apart from God. “Would he command you to disbelieve after you have submitted to God,” that is, yielded to His ordinances? One account gives this occasion for the verse’s revelation: the Christians of Najrān said, “Muḥammad, do you want us to worship you and take you as a lord?” The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, answered: “God forbid that we worship anyone other than God, or command the worship of another.” Another says that a man asked: “Messenger of God, we greet you as we greet one another; should we not prostrate ourselves before you?” He answered: “No one should prostrate himself before anyone apart from God. Rather, honour your prophet and acknowledge the due of those to whom it belongs.”"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Honouring the guide without making the intermediary the goal","html":"<p>The spiritually poor have always venerated their shaykhs, going so far as to kiss their feet and the ground before them, and exerting themselves in their service. When the shaykhs see them doing so, they do not forbid them, because this is the disciples’ gain and a cause of their spiritual opening (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفتح<\/bdi>). Of this someone has said: <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N044\">[XIV-N044]<\/a><\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Through the slaughter of the lower selves and the lowering of heads,<br/>the cups are made clear.<\/blockquote><p>Yet the shaykhs guide them to the Presence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحضرة<\/bdi>), until they efface them from witnessing the intermediary (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الواسطة<\/bdi>). Their veneration and lowering of their heads are then for God, not another. At that point they become rabbānīs: knowers of God, brought near. Our shaykh used to say: “Do not visit me on the basis that I am your shaykh. Rather, recognize [God] in us and be effaced from seeing our sensible appearance, so that the veneration is for God, our Lord.” End of the quotation. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N045\">[XIV-N045]<\/a><\/p><p>Thus, when shaykhs direct the spiritually poor to veneration and right conduct (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأدب<\/bdi>), they do not intend this for their own persons—far be it from them! It is not for a human being whom God gives special spiritual distinction then to tell people, “Be servants of me apart from God.” Rather, he tells them: “Be rabbānīs, knowers of God,” so that your veneration is for God alone. Nor does he command separation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفرق<\/bdi>), so that they take things as lords apart from God. Rather, he commands gathering in unity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الجمع<\/bdi>), so that they become absent from all that is other than God. How could he command separation when he is pointing them to gathering? Would he command them to disbelieve after they had submitted? And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The spiritually poor have always venerated their shaykhs, going so far as to kiss their feet and the ground before them, and exerting themselves in their service. When the shaykhs see them doing so, they do not forbid them, because this is the disciples’ gain and a cause of their spiritual opening ( الفتح ). Of this someone has said: [XIV-N044] Through the slaughter of the lower selves and the lowering of heads, the cups are made clear. Yet the shaykhs guide them to the Presence ( الحضرة ), until they efface them from witnessing the intermediary ( الواسطة ). Their veneration and lowering of their heads are then for God, not another. At that point they become rabbānīs: knowers of God, brought near. Our shaykh used to say: “Do not visit me on the basis that I am your shaykh. Rather, recognize [God] in us and be effaced from seeing our sensible appearance, so that the veneration is for God, our Lord.” End of the quotation. [XIV-N045] Thus, when shaykhs direct the spiritually poor to veneration and right conduct ( الأدب ), they do not intend this for their own persons—far be it from them! It is not for a human being whom God gives special spiritual distinction then to tell people, “Be servants of me apart from God.” Rather, he tells them: “Be rabbānīs, knowers of God,” so that your veneration is for God alone. Nor does he command separation ( الفرق ), so that they take things as lords apart from God. Rather, he commands gathering in unity ( الجمع ), so that they become absent from all that is other than God. How could he command separation when he is pointing them to gathering? Would he command them to disbelieve after they had submitted? And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then mentioned the covenant taken from the prophets and their communities to believe in the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then mentioned the covenant taken from the prophets and their communities to believe in the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C374.1","page":374,"ar":"هذا مشروط كما بين الشيخ مرارا - بأن يكون في حدود الشرع الشريف.","en":"This is conditional—as the shaykh has repeatedly explained—on remaining within the limits of the noble Sacred Law.","anchor":"ويجتهدون في خدمتهم"}],"english_characters":4503,"arabic_characters":3056},{"id":"q3-81-82","q":"3:81–82","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:81–82 · The prophetic covenant and the inseparability of Law and Reality","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 374–375","cairo":"I:374–375","source_page_first":374,"source_page_last":375,"ar":"وَإِذْ أَخَذَ اللَّهُ مِيثاقَ النَّبِيِّينَ لَما آتَيْتُكُمْ مِنْ كِتابٍ وَحِكْمَةٍ ثُمَّ جاءَكُمْ رَسُولٌ مُصَدِّقٌ لِما مَعَكُمْ لَتُؤْمِنُنَّ بِهِ وَلَتَنْصُرُنَّهُ قالَ أَأَقْرَرْتُمْ وَأَخَذْتُمْ عَلى ذلِكُمْ إِصْرِي قالُوا أَقْرَرْنا قالَ فَاشْهَدُوا وَأَنَا مَعَكُمْ مِنَ الشَّاهِدِينَ (81) فَمَنْ تَوَلَّى بَعْدَ ذلِكَ فَأُولئِكَ هُمُ الْفاسِقُونَ (82)\nقلت: اللام في (لما) ، موطئة للقسم لأن أخذ الميثاق بمعنى الاستحلاف، و (ما) : يحتمل الشرطية، و (لتؤمنن) : جواب القسم، سد مسد الجواب، أي: مهما آتيناكم من كتاب وحكمة ثم جاءكم رسول الله لتؤمنن به.\nويحتمل الموصولية، و (لتؤمنن) : خبر عنه، وحذف شرط يدل عليه السياق أي: للذي آتيناكم من كتاب وحكمة، ثم إذا جاءكم رسول مصدق لما معكم لتؤمنن به. ومن قرأ بكسر اللام كان تعليلاً للأمر بالإيمان بالرسول، أي:\nلأجل الذي خصصتكم به إذا جاءكُمْ رسول مصدق لما معكم لتؤمنن به، وإذا كان أخذ الله الميثاق على الأنبياء كان على الأتباع أولى، أو استغنى بذكر الأنبياء عن ذكر أتباعهم لأنهم فى حكمهم.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: واذكر إِذْ أخذنا الميثاق على النبيين من لدن آدم عليه السلام إلى عيسى عليه السلام. وقلنا لهم: والله للذي خصصتكم به مِنْ كِتابٍ وَحِكْمَةٍ، ثم إن ظهر رسول مُصَدِّقٌ لِما مَعَكُمْ لَتُؤْمِنُنَّ بِهِ وَلَتَنْصُرُنَّهُ أنتم وأممكم، أو: لأجل الذي خصصتكم به مما تقدم لئن أدركتم محمداً لتؤمنن به ولتنصرنه. قال سيدنا علي- كرّم الله وجهه-: (لم يبعث اللهُ نبياً، آدم ومن بعده، إلا أخذ عليه العهد في محمد، وأمره بأخذ العهد على قومه ليُؤْمنُنَّ به، ولئن بُعث وهم أحياءٌ لينْصُرُنَّه) .\nقالَ الحق جلّ جلاله لمَن أخذ عليهم العهد: أَأَقْرَرْتُمْ بذلك وقبلتموه، وَأَخَذْتُمْ عَلى ذلِكُمْ إِصْرِي أي:\nعهدي وميثاقي؟ قالُوا أَقْرَرْنا وقبلنا، قالَ فَاشْهَدُوا على أنفسكم، أو ليشهدْ بعضكم على بعض بالإقرار، أو فاشهدوا يا ملائكتي عليهم، وَأَنَا مَعَكُمْ مِنَ الشَّاهِدِينَ، وفيه توكيد وتحذير عظيم، فَمَنْ تَوَلَّى بَعْدَ ذلِكَ الإقرار والشهادة، وأعرض عن الإيمان به، ونصره بعد ظهوره، فَأُولئِكَ هُمُ الْفاسِقُونَ الخارجون عن الإيمان المتمردون في الكفران.\nالإشارة: كما أخذ الله العهد على الأنبياء وأممهم في الإيمان به عليه الصلاة والسلام، أخذ الميثاق على العلماء وأتباعهم من العامة، لئن أدركوا وليّاً من أولياء الله، حاملاً لواء الحقيقة، مصدقا لما معهم من الشريعة، ليؤمنن به ولينصرنه، فمن تولى وأعرض عن الإذعان إليهم فأولئك هم الفاسقون الخارجون عن دائرة الولاية، محرومون من سابق العناية، فإن الحقيقة إنما هي لب الشريعة وخلاصتها، فإنما مثل الحقيقة والشريعة كالروح للجسد، فالشريعة كالجسد، والحقيقة كالروح، فالشريعة بلا حقيقة جسد بلا روح، والحقيقة بلا شريعة روح بلا جسد، فلا قيام لهذا إلا بهذا، فمن تشرَع ولم يتحقق فقد تفسق، ومن تحقق ولم يتشرّع فقد تزندق، ومَن جمع بينهما فقد تحقق، ومن خرج عنهما فقد خرج عن دين الله وطلب غيره. وإليه توجه الإنكار بقوله:","quran_ar":"وَإِذْ أَخَذَ اللَّهُ مِيثاقَ النَّبِيِّينَ لَما آتَيْتُكُمْ مِنْ كِتابٍ وَحِكْمَةٍ ثُمَّ جاءَكُمْ رَسُولٌ مُصَدِّقٌ لِما مَعَكُمْ لَتُؤْمِنُنَّ بِهِ وَلَتَنْصُرُنَّهُ قالَ أَأَقْرَرْتُمْ وَأَخَذْتُمْ عَلى ذلِكُمْ إِصْرِي قالُوا أَقْرَرْنا قالَ فَاشْهَدُوا وَأَنَا مَعَكُمْ مِنَ الشَّاهِدِينَ (81) فَمَنْ تَوَلَّى بَعْدَ ذلِكَ فَأُولئِكَ هُمُ الْفاسِقُونَ (82)","quran_en":"81. When God took the covenant of the prophets: “Whatever scripture and wisdom I give you, then a messenger comes to you confirming what is with you, you shall surely believe in him and support him.” He said: “Do you acknowledge this and accept My binding covenant upon it?” They said: “We acknowledge it.” He said: “Then bear witness; I am with you among the witnesses.” 82. Whoever turns away after that—it is they who are the transgressors.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Oath, condition and the binding covenant","html":"<p>I say: the lām in la-mā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لما<\/bdi>) prepares for an oath (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">موطئة للقسم<\/bdi>), since taking the covenant has the force of exacting an oath (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الاستحلاف<\/bdi>). Mā may be conditional (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شرطية<\/bdi>); “you shall surely believe” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لتؤمنن<\/bdi>) is the response to the oath (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">جواب القسم<\/bdi>), also taking the place of the response to the condition. Thus: whatever scripture and wisdom We give you, then a messenger of God comes to you, you shall surely believe in him. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N046\">[XIV-N046]<\/a><\/p><p>Mā may instead be relative (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">موصولية<\/bdi>), with “you shall surely believe” as its predicate (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خبر<\/bdi>). A condition indicated by the context has been omitted: with respect to the scripture and wisdom I have given you, then, if a messenger comes confirming what you have, you shall surely believe in him. In the reading with a kasra on the lām, the phrase supplies the reason for the command to believe in the messenger: because of what I have specially granted you, when a messenger comes confirming what you have, you shall surely believe in him. If God took the covenant from the prophets, it applies all the more to their followers; alternatively, mentioning the prophets suffices instead of mentioning their followers, since they fall under the same ruling.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: the lām in la-mā ( لما ) prepares for an oath ( موطئة للقسم ), since taking the covenant has the force of exacting an oath ( الاستحلاف ). Mā may be conditional ( شرطية ); “you shall surely believe” ( لتؤمنن ) is the response to the oath ( جواب القسم ), also taking the place of the response to the condition. Thus: whatever scripture and wisdom We give you, then a messenger of God comes to you, you shall surely believe in him. [XIV-N046] Mā may instead be relative ( موصولية ), with “you shall surely believe” as its predicate ( خبر ). A condition indicated by the context has been omitted: with respect to the scripture and wisdom I have given you, then, if a messenger comes confirming what you have, you shall surely believe in him. In the reading with a kasra on the lām, the phrase supplies the reason for the command to believe in the messenger: because of what I have specially granted you, when a messenger comes confirming what you have, you shall surely believe in him. If God took the covenant from the prophets, it applies all the more to their followers; alternatively, mentioning the prophets suffices instead of mentioning their followers, since they fall under the same ruling."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Acknowledgment, support and mutual witness","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: remember when We took the covenant from the prophets, from Adam to Jesus, peace be upon them. We told them: by God, with respect to the scripture and wisdom with which I have specially favoured you, should a messenger appear “confirming what is with you, you shall surely believe in him and support him”—you and your communities. Alternatively: because of the favour already described, should you encounter Muḥammad, you shall surely believe in him and support him.<\/p><p>Our master ʿAlī, God ennoble his face, said: “God sent no prophet, Adam or any after him, without taking his pledge concerning Muḥammad and commanding him to take his people’s pledge that they would believe in him, and that, should he be sent while they were alive, they would support him.”<\/p><p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, said to those from whom He took the pledge: “Do you acknowledge” and accept it, “and accept My binding covenant (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إصري<\/bdi>) upon it”—My pledge and covenant? “They said: ‘We acknowledge’” and accept. He said: “Then bear witness” against yourselves, or let each bear witness to the others’ acknowledgment; or, bear witness against them, My angels. “I am with you among the witnesses.” This contains emphatic confirmation and a mighty warning. “Whoever turns away after” that acknowledgment and witnessing, refusing belief in him and support for him after his appearance, “it is they who are the transgressors”: departing from faith and rebelliously persisting in unbelief.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: remember when We took the covenant from the prophets, from Adam to Jesus, peace be upon them. We told them: by God, with respect to the scripture and wisdom with which I have specially favoured you, should a messenger appear “confirming what is with you, you shall surely believe in him and support him”—you and your communities. Alternatively: because of the favour already described, should you encounter Muḥammad, you shall surely believe in him and support him. Our master ʿAlī, God ennoble his face, said: “God sent no prophet, Adam or any after him, without taking his pledge concerning Muḥammad and commanding him to take his people’s pledge that they would believe in him, and that, should he be sent while they were alive, they would support him.” The Real, exalted is His majesty, said to those from whom He took the pledge: “Do you acknowledge” and accept it, “and accept My binding covenant ( إصري ) upon it”—My pledge and covenant? “They said: ‘We acknowledge’” and accept. He said: “Then bear witness” against yourselves, or let each bear witness to the others’ acknowledgment; or, bear witness against them, My angels. “I am with you among the witnesses.” This contains emphatic confirmation and a mighty warning. “Whoever turns away after” that acknowledgment and witnessing, refusing belief in him and support for him after his appearance, “it is they who are the transgressors”: departing from faith and rebelliously persisting in unbelief."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The religious Law as body and Reality as spirit","html":"<p>Just as God took the pledge from the prophets and their communities to believe in him, blessings and peace be upon him, He took a covenant from the scholars and their ordinary followers: should they encounter one of God’s friends bearing the banner of Reality (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحقيقة<\/bdi>), confirming the religious Law (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الشريعة<\/bdi>) they possess, they must believe in him and support him. Those who turn away and refuse to yield to them are the transgressors, outside the circle of friendship with God (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الولاية<\/bdi>) and deprived of antecedent divine care (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">سابق العناية<\/bdi>).<\/p><p>Reality is the very kernel and distillation of the Law. Their relation is that of spirit to body: the Law is like the body, Reality like the spirit. Law without Reality is a body without spirit; Reality without Law is a spirit without body. Neither can stand without the other. Whoever adheres to the Law without realizing Reality falls into transgression; whoever realizes Reality without adhering to the Law falls into heresy; whoever brings both together attains realization. Whoever departs from both has departed from God’s religion and sought another. To him is directed the reproach in His words:<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Just as God took the pledge from the prophets and their communities to believe in him, blessings and peace be upon him, He took a covenant from the scholars and their ordinary followers: should they encounter one of God’s friends bearing the banner of Reality ( الحقيقة ), confirming the religious Law ( الشريعة ) they possess, they must believe in him and support him. Those who turn away and refuse to yield to them are the transgressors, outside the circle of friendship with God ( الولاية ) and deprived of antecedent divine care ( سابق العناية ). Reality is the very kernel and distillation of the Law. Their relation is that of spirit to body: the Law is like the body, Reality like the spirit. Law without Reality is a body without spirit; Reality without Law is a spirit without body. Neither can stand without the other. Whoever adheres to the Law without realizing Reality falls into transgression; whoever realizes Reality without adhering to the Law falls into heresy; whoever brings both together attains realization. Whoever departs from both has departed from God’s religion and sought another. To him is directed the reproach in His words:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3855,"arabic_characters":2576},{"id":"q3-83","q":"3:83","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:83 · Willing and unwilling submission; outward obedience and inward acceptance","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 375–376","cairo":"I:375–376","source_page_first":375,"source_page_last":376,"ar":"أَفَغَيْرَ دِينِ اللَّهِ يَبْغُونَ وَلَهُ أَسْلَمَ مَنْ فِي السَّماواتِ وَالْأَرْضِ طَوْعاً وَكَرْهاً وَإِلَيْهِ يُرْجَعُونَ (83)\nقلت: (أفغير) : مفعول مقدم، و (يبغون) : معطوف على محذوف، أي: أتتولون فتبغون غير دين الله، وقدم المعمول لأنه المقصود بالإنكار، و (طوعاً وكرهاً) : حالان، أي: طائعين أو كارهين.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله للنصارى واليهود، لمَّا اختصموا إلى النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم، وادعوا أن كل واحد على دين إبراهيم، فقال لهم- عليه الصلاة والسلام: «كِلاكما بَرِيءٌ مِنْ دِينه، وأنا على دِينه، فخذوا به» ، فغضبوا، وقالوا:\nوالله لا نرضى بحكمك ولا نأخذ بدينك، فقال لهم الحق جل جلاله- منكرا عليهم-: أفتبغون غير دين الله الذي ارتضاه لخليله وحبيبه، وقد انقاد له تعالى مَنْ فِي السَّماواتِ وَالْأَرْضِ طائعين ومكرهين، فأهل السموات\nانقادوا طائعين، وأهل الأرض منهم من انقاد طوعاً بالنظر واتباع الحجة أو بغيرها، ومنهم من انقاد كرهاً أو بمعاينة ما يُلجئ إلى الإسلام كنتق الجبل وإدراك الغرق والإشراف على الموت، أو: «طوعاً» كالملائكة والمؤمنين، فإنهم انقادوا لما يراد منهم طوعاً، (وكرهاً) كالكفار فانقادوا لما يراد منهم كرهاً، وكلٍّ إليه راجعون، لا يخرج عن دائرة حكمه، أو راجعون إليه بالبعث والنشور. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: اعلم أن الدين الحقيقي هو الانقياد إلى الله في الظاهر والباطن، أما الانقياد إلى الله في الظاهر فيكون بامتثال أمره واجتناب نهيه، وأما الانقياد إلى الله في الباطن فيكون بالرضى بحكمه والاستسلام لقهره.\nفكل من قصَّر في الانقياد في الظاهر، أو تسخط من الأحكام الجلالية في الباطن، فقد خرج عن كمال الدين، فيقال له: أفغير دين الله تبغون وقد انقاد له (من في السموات والأرض طوعاً وكرهاً) ، فإما أن تنقاد طوعاً أو ترجع إليه كرها. وفي بعض الآثار يقول الله تبارك وتعالى: «منْ لم يَرْضَ بقضائي ولم يَصْبِرْ على بَلائِي، فليخرجْ من تحت سَمَائي، وليتخذْ ربّاً سِوَاي» .\nوسبب تبرّم القلب عن نزول الأحكام القهرية مرضُه وضعف نور يقينه، فكل من استنكف عن صحبة الطبيب، فله من هذا العتاب حظ ونصيب، فالأولياء حجة الله على العلماء، والعلماء حجة الله على العوام، فمن لم يستقم ظاهره عُوتب على تفريطه في صحبة العلماء، ومن لم يستقم باطنه عاتبه الله تعالى على ترك صُحبة الأولياء، أعني العارفين. وبالله التوفيق، وهو الهادي إلى سواء الطريق.\nثم بين الحق- تعالى- حقيقة الإيمان والإسلام الذي يجب اتباعه على جميع الأنام، فقال:","quran_ar":"أَفَغَيْرَ دِينِ اللَّهِ يَبْغُونَ وَلَهُ أَسْلَمَ مَنْ فِي السَّماواتِ وَالْأَرْضِ طَوْعاً وَكَرْهاً وَإِلَيْهِ يُرْجَعُونَ (83)","quran_en":"83. Do they seek a religion other than God’s, when to Him has submitted whoever is in the heavens and the earth, willingly or unwillingly, and to Him they will be returned?","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The fronted object and the circumstantial expressions","html":"<p>I say: “other than” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أفغير<\/bdi>) is a fronted object (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مفعول مقدّم<\/bdi>). “They seek” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">يبغون<\/bdi>) is coordinated with an omitted expression: do you turn away and so seek a religion other than God’s? The governed expression (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المعمول<\/bdi>) is placed first because it is the target of the reproach. “Willingly and unwillingly” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">طوعاً وكرهاً<\/bdi>) are two circumstantial expressions (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حالان<\/bdi>): being willing or unwilling.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “other than” ( أفغير ) is a fronted object ( مفعول مقدّم ). “They seek” ( يبغون ) is coordinated with an omitted expression: do you turn away and so seek a religion other than God’s? The governed expression ( المعمول ) is placed first because it is the target of the reproach. “Willingly and unwillingly” ( طوعاً وكرهاً ) are two circumstantial expressions ( حالان ): being willing or unwilling."},{"type":"exposition","title":"No creature stands outside God’s rule","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, addresses the Christians and Jews when they brought their dispute to the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, each claiming to follow Abraham’s religion. He told them: “You are both disassociated from his religion; I follow his religion, so take hold of it.” They grew angry and said: “By God, we will neither accept your judgment nor adopt your religion.” The Real, exalted is His majesty, rebukes them: do you seek a religion other than God’s, which He approved for His intimate friend and His beloved, when whoever is in the heavens and the earth has yielded to Him, willingly or under compulsion?<\/p><p>The inhabitants of the heavens yield willingly. Of the inhabitants of the earth, some yield willingly through reflection and following proof, or by another means; others yield unwillingly, or through witnessing what compels submission, such as the raising of the mountain, being overtaken by drowning, or approaching death. Alternatively, “willingly” describes the angels and believers, who willingly submit to what is required of them; “unwillingly” describes the unbelievers, who unwillingly submit to what is willed concerning them. All return to Him: none stands outside the circle of His judgment. Alternatively, they return to Him through resurrection and the raising of the dead. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, addresses the Christians and Jews when they brought their dispute to the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, each claiming to follow Abraham’s religion. He told them: “You are both disassociated from his religion; I follow his religion, so take hold of it.” They grew angry and said: “By God, we will neither accept your judgment nor adopt your religion.” The Real, exalted is His majesty, rebukes them: do you seek a religion other than God’s, which He approved for His intimate friend and His beloved, when whoever is in the heavens and the earth has yielded to Him, willingly or under compulsion? The inhabitants of the heavens yield willingly. Of the inhabitants of the earth, some yield willingly through reflection and following proof, or by another means; others yield unwillingly, or through witnessing what compels submission, such as the raising of the mountain, being overtaken by drowning, or approaching death. Alternatively, “willingly” describes the angels and believers, who willingly submit to what is required of them; “unwillingly” describes the unbelievers, who unwillingly submit to what is willed concerning them. All return to Him: none stands outside the circle of His judgment. Alternatively, they return to Him through resurrection and the raising of the dead. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The two forms of yielding and the physician of the heart","html":"<p>Know that true religion is yielding to God outwardly and inwardly. Outward yielding consists in fulfilling His commands and avoiding His prohibitions. Inward yielding consists in contentment with His judgment (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الرضا بحكمه<\/bdi>) and surrender to His overpowering might (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الاستسلام لقهره<\/bdi>).<\/p><p>Whoever falls short in outward obedience or inwardly resents the majestic decrees (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأحكام الجلالية<\/bdi>) has fallen short of the perfection of religion. He is asked: do you seek a religion other than God’s, when whoever is in the heavens and earth has yielded to Him, willingly or unwillingly? Either yield willingly or return to Him unwillingly. In certain transmitted reports, God—blessed and exalted—says: “Whoever is not content with My decree and does not patiently bear My trial, let him leave the shelter of My heaven and take a lord other than Me.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N047\">[XIV-N047]<\/a><\/p><p>The reason the heart chafes at the descent of overpowering decrees is its sickness and the weakness of the light of its certainty (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اليقين<\/bdi>). Whoever disdains the physician’s companionship has a share in this reproach. God’s friends are His proof against the scholars; the scholars are His proof against ordinary people. Whoever’s outward conduct is not upright is reproached for neglecting the companionship of the scholars. Whoever’s inward state is not upright is reproached by God Most High for abandoning the companionship of the friends of God—that is, the knowers of Him. Success comes through God, and He guides to the even way.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Know that true religion is yielding to God outwardly and inwardly. Outward yielding consists in fulfilling His commands and avoiding His prohibitions. Inward yielding consists in contentment with His judgment ( الرضا بحكمه ) and surrender to His overpowering might ( الاستسلام لقهره ). Whoever falls short in outward obedience or inwardly resents the majestic decrees ( الأحكام الجلالية ) has fallen short of the perfection of religion. He is asked: do you seek a religion other than God’s, when whoever is in the heavens and earth has yielded to Him, willingly or unwillingly? Either yield willingly or return to Him unwillingly. In certain transmitted reports, God—blessed and exalted—says: “Whoever is not content with My decree and does not patiently bear My trial, let him leave the shelter of My heaven and take a lord other than Me.” [XIV-N047] The reason the heart chafes at the descent of overpowering decrees is its sickness and the weakness of the light of its certainty ( اليقين ). Whoever disdains the physician’s companionship has a share in this reproach. God’s friends are His proof against the scholars; the scholars are His proof against ordinary people. Whoever’s outward conduct is not upright is reproached for neglecting the companionship of the scholars. Whoever’s inward state is not upright is reproached by God Most High for abandoning the companionship of the friends of God—that is, the knowers of Him. Success comes through God, and He guides to the even way."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real Most High then explained the reality of faith and submission that all people must follow, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real Most High then explained the reality of faith and submission that all people must follow, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3352,"arabic_characters":2103},{"id":"q3-84","q":"3:84","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:84 · Faith in all the prophets and respect for the friends of God","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 376–377","cairo":"I:376–377","source_page_first":376,"source_page_last":377,"ar":"قُلْ آمَنَّا بِاللَّهِ وَما أُنْزِلَ عَلَيْنا وَما أُنْزِلَ عَلى إِبْراهِيمَ وَإِسْماعِيلَ وَإِسْحاقَ وَيَعْقُوبَ وَالْأَسْباطِ وَما أُوتِيَ مُوسى وَعِيسى وَالنَّبِيُّونَ مِنْ رَبِّهِمْ لاَ نُفَرِّقُ بَيْنَ أَحَدٍ مِنْهُمْ وَنَحْنُ لَهُ مُسْلِمُونَ (84)\nقلت: (أُنزل) : يتعدى بإلى لأنه ينتهي إلى الرسل، ويتعدى بعلى، لأنه يأتي من ناحية العلو والاستعلاء، وفرَّق بعضهُم بين التعبير هنا بعلى وفي البقرة بإلى، فقال: لأن الخطاب هنا للرسول بالخصوص، وقد أنزل عليه الوحي مباشرة، وهناك الخطاب للمسلمين، وإنما أنزل الوحي متوجهاً إليهم بالواسطة، ولم يكن عليهم بالمباشرة.\nوالله تعالى أعلم.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: قُلْ يا محمد لأهل الكتاب الذين فرقوا في إيمانهم بين الرسل: أما نحن فقد آمنا بالذي أُنْزِلَ عَلَيْنا وَما أُنْزِلَ على جميع الأنبياء والرسل لاَ نُفَرِّقُ بَيْنَ أَحَدٍ مِنْهُمْ كما فرَّقتم أنتم، فَضَلَلْتُم، وَنَحْنُ لَهُ مُسْلِمُونَ أي: منقادون لأحكامه الظاهرة والباطنة، أو مخلصون في أعمالنا كلها، وقدَّم المنزل علينا على المنزل على غيرنا، لأنه عيار عليه ومُعَرَّفٌ به. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: ينبغي للفقير أن يبالغ في تعظيم شيخه، ويسوغ له التغالي في شأنه ما لم يخرجه عن طَوْر البشر، وما لم يؤد ذلك إلى إسقاط حُرمة غيره من الأولياء بالتنقيص أو غيره، فحرمة الأولياء كحرمة الأنبياء، فمن فرق بينهم حُرِم بركة جميعهم. وبالله التوفيق.\nثم إن ملة الإسلام التي جاء بها نبينا- عليه الصلاة والسلام- هى التي أحرزت هذا الاعتقاد الصحيح، فكل من خرج عنها فقد ضل عن الحق الصريح، كما أشار إلى ذلك الحق تعالى بقوله:","quran_ar":"قُلْ آمَنَّا بِاللَّهِ وَما أُنْزِلَ عَلَيْنا وَما أُنْزِلَ عَلى إِبْراهِيمَ وَإِسْماعِيلَ وَإِسْحاقَ وَيَعْقُوبَ وَالْأَسْباطِ وَما أُوتِيَ مُوسى وَعِيسى وَالنَّبِيُّونَ مِنْ رَبِّهِمْ لاَ نُفَرِّقُ بَيْنَ أَحَدٍ مِنْهُمْ وَنَحْنُ لَهُ مُسْلِمُونَ (84)","quran_en":"84. Say: “We believe in God, in what has been sent down upon us, in what was sent down upon Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob and the Tribes, and in what Moses, Jesus and the prophets were given from their Lord. We make no distinction between any of them, and to Him we submit.”","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Revelation “upon” and “to” its recipients","html":"<p>I say: unzila (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أُنزل<\/bdi>), “was sent down,” takes ilā because revelation reaches the messengers, and takes ʿalā because it comes from above, with the sense of being over them. Some distinguish the use of ʿalā here from ilā in al-Baqara [2:136]: here the address is specifically to the Messenger, and revelation was sent down upon him directly; there the address is to the Muslims, and revelation was directed to them through an intermediary, not sent down directly upon them. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: unzila ( أُنزل ), “was sent down,” takes ilā because revelation reaches the messengers, and takes ʿalā because it comes from above, with the sense of being over them. Some distinguish the use of ʿalā here from ilā in al-Baqara [2:136]: here the address is specifically to the Messenger, and revelation was sent down upon him directly; there the address is to the Muslims, and revelation was directed to them through an intermediary, not sent down directly upon them. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Acknowledging revelation without selective rejection","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Say,” Muḥammad, to the People of the Book who distinguish between messengers in their belief: as for us, we believe in what was sent down upon us and in what was sent down upon all the prophets and messengers. “We make no distinction between any of them,” as you have distinguished and so gone astray. “To Him we submit”: yield to His outward and inward ordinances; or, devote all our actions sincerely to Him. What was sent down upon us is placed before what was sent down upon others because it is the criterion by which the latter is measured and the means by which it is made known. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Say,” Muḥammad, to the People of the Book who distinguish between messengers in their belief: as for us, we believe in what was sent down upon us and in what was sent down upon all the prophets and messengers. “We make no distinction between any of them,” as you have distinguished and so gone astray. “To Him we submit”: yield to His outward and inward ordinances; or, devote all our actions sincerely to Him. What was sent down upon us is placed before what was sent down upon others because it is the criterion by which the latter is measured and the means by which it is made known. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Veneration without denigrating other saints","html":"<p>The spiritually poor person should greatly venerate his shaykh. He may go far in esteeming him, so long as he does not raise him beyond the human condition or thereby violate the honour of other friends of God through disparagement or anything else. The inviolability of the friends of God is like that of the prophets: whoever discriminates between them is deprived of the blessing of them all. Success comes through God.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The spiritually poor person should greatly venerate his shaykh. He may go far in esteeming him, so long as he does not raise him beyond the human condition or thereby violate the honour of other friends of God through disparagement or anything else. The inviolability of the friends of God is like that of the prophets: whoever discriminates between them is deprived of the blessing of them all. Success comes through God."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Islamic religious way brought by our Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, is the one that has secured this sound belief. Whoever departs from it has strayed from manifest truth, as the Real Most High indicates, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Islamic religious way brought by our Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, is the one that has secured this sound belief. Whoever departs from it has strayed from manifest truth, as the Real Most High indicates, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1809,"arabic_characters":1397},{"id":"q3-85-86","q":"3:85–86","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:85–86 · Turning from recognized truth and the corruption of the primordial disposition","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 377–378","cairo":"I:377–378","source_page_first":377,"source_page_last":378,"ar":"وَمَنْ يَبْتَغِ غَيْرَ الْإِسْلامِ دِيناً فَلَنْ يُقْبَلَ مِنْهُ وَهُوَ فِي الْآخِرَةِ مِنَ الْخاسِرِينَ (85) كَيْفَ يَهْدِي اللَّهُ قَوْماً كَفَرُوا بَعْدَ إِيمانِهِمْ وَشَهِدُوا أَنَّ الرَّسُولَ حَقٌّ وَجاءَهُمُ الْبَيِّناتُ وَاللَّهُ لا يَهْدِي الْقَوْمَ الظَّالِمِينَ (86)\nقلت: (وشهدوا) : عطف على ما في (إيمانهم) من معنى الفعل، والتقدير: بعد أن آمنوا وشهدوا.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله لرجال من الأنصار ارتدوا عن الإسلام ولحقوا بمكة، منهم الحارث بن سويد الأنصاري: وَمَنْ يطلب غَيْرَ الْإِسْلامِ دِيناً يتدين به فَلَنْ يُقْبَلَ مِنْهُ أبداً، وَهُوَ فِي الْآخِرَةِ مِنَ الْخاسِرِينَ لأنه أبطل الفطرة السليمة التي فطر الناس عليها، واستبدلها بالتقليد الرديء، بعد أن عاين سواطع البرهان، وشهدت نفسه بالحق والبيان، ولذلك وقع التعجب والاستبعاد من هدايته فقال: كَيْفَ يَهْدِي اللَّهُ قَوْماً كَفَرُوا بعد أن آمنوا، وَشَهِدُوا أَنَّ الرَّسُولَ حَقٌّ وَجاءَهُمُ الْبَيِّناتُ أي: المعجزات الواضحات، فإن الحائد عن الحق بعد ما وضح، منهمك في الضلال، بعيد عن الرشاد، فقد ظلم نفسه وبخسها، وَاللَّهُ لا يَهْدِي الْقَوْمَ الظَّالِمِينَ الذين ظلموا أنفسهم بالإخلال بالنظر، ووضعوا الكفر موضع الإيمان، ولعل هذا في قوم مخصوصين سبق لهم الشقاء.\nثم ذكر جزاءهم، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَمَنْ يَبْتَغِ غَيْرَ الْإِسْلامِ دِيناً فَلَنْ يُقْبَلَ مِنْهُ وَهُوَ فِي الْآخِرَةِ مِنَ الْخاسِرِينَ (85) كَيْفَ يَهْدِي اللَّهُ قَوْماً كَفَرُوا بَعْدَ إِيمانِهِمْ وَشَهِدُوا أَنَّ الرَّسُولَ حَقٌّ وَجاءَهُمُ الْبَيِّناتُ وَاللَّهُ لا يَهْدِي الْقَوْمَ الظَّالِمِينَ (86)","quran_en":"85. Whoever seeks a religion other than submission to God—it will not be accepted from him, and in the Hereafter he will be among the losers. 86. How shall God guide a people who disbelieved after their belief, after bearing witness that the Messenger is true, and after the clear proofs came to them? God does not guide the wrongdoing people.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Coordination with the verbal meaning of a noun","html":"<p>I say: “and they bore witness” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وشهدوا<\/bdi>) is coordinated with the verbal meaning contained in “their belief” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إيمانهم<\/bdi>). The reconstructed sense is: after they believed and bore witness.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “and they bore witness” ( وشهدوا ) is coordinated with the verbal meaning contained in “their belief” ( إيمانهم ). The reconstructed sense is: after they believed and bore witness."},{"type":"theology","title":"Rejecting proof after acknowledging it","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, addresses some men of the Helpers who renounced Islam and went to Mecca, including al-Ḥārith ibn Suwayd al-Anṣārī: whoever seeks “a religion other than Islam” to profess will never have it accepted from him; “in the Hereafter he will be among the losers.” He has nullified the sound primordial disposition (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفطرة السليمة<\/bdi>) upon which people were created, exchanging it for corrupt imitation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التقليد الرديء<\/bdi>), after beholding the blazing proofs and inwardly bearing witness to the truth and its clarity.<\/p><p>Thus the question expresses astonishment at, and the remoteness of, his being guided: “How shall God guide a people who disbelieved” after believing, “bore witness that the Messenger is true, and received the clear proofs”—the manifest miracles? One who turns from truth after it becomes clear is immersed in error and far from right guidance. He has wronged and diminished his own soul. “God does not guide the wrongdoing people,” who wrong themselves by defective reflection and put unbelief in the place of faith. Perhaps this concerns a particular people whose wretchedness was already decreed.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, addresses some men of the Helpers who renounced Islam and went to Mecca, including al-Ḥārith ibn Suwayd al-Anṣārī: whoever seeks “a religion other than Islam” to profess will never have it accepted from him; “in the Hereafter he will be among the losers.” He has nullified the sound primordial disposition ( الفطرة السليمة ) upon which people were created, exchanging it for corrupt imitation ( التقليد الرديء ), after beholding the blazing proofs and inwardly bearing witness to the truth and its clarity. Thus the question expresses astonishment at, and the remoteness of, his being guided: “How shall God guide a people who disbelieved” after believing, “bore witness that the Messenger is true, and received the clear proofs”—the manifest miracles? One who turns from truth after it becomes clear is immersed in error and far from right guidance. He has wronged and diminished his own soul. “God does not guide the wrongdoing people,” who wrong themselves by defective reflection and put unbelief in the place of faith. Perhaps this concerns a particular people whose wretchedness was already decreed."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then mentioned their recompense, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then mentioned their recompense, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1369,"arabic_characters":1137},{"id":"q3-87-88","q":"3:87–88","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:87–88 · The consequence of rejection and the opening towards repentance","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 378–378","cairo":"I:378","source_page_first":378,"source_page_last":378,"ar":"أُولئِكَ جَزاؤُهُمْ أَنَّ عَلَيْهِمْ لَعْنَةَ اللَّهِ وَالْمَلائِكَةِ وَالنَّاسِ أَجْمَعِينَ (87) خالِدِينَ فِيها لا يُخَفَّفُ عَنْهُمُ الْعَذابُ وَلا هُمْ يُنْظَرُونَ (88)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: أُولئِكَ المرتدون عن الإسلام- جَزاؤُهُمْ: أن تلعنهم الملائكة والناسُ أجمعون، مؤمنُهم وكافرهم، لأن الكافر يلعنُ من ترك دين الحق، وإنْ كان لا يشعر بمن هو على الحقّ. خالِدِينَ في اللعنة، أو في النار، لدلالة السياق عليها، أو في العقوبة. لا يُخَفَّفُ عَنْهُمُ الْعَذابُ ساعة، ولا هم يُمهلون عنها لحظة.\nثم إنَّ الحارث نَدِم، وأرسل إلى قومه أن اسألوا الرسول صلّى الله عليه وسلم، هل لي من توبة؟ فنزل قوله تعالى:","quran_ar":"أُولئِكَ جَزاؤُهُمْ أَنَّ عَلَيْهِمْ لَعْنَةَ اللَّهِ وَالْمَلائِكَةِ وَالنَّاسِ أَجْمَعِينَ (87) خالِدِينَ فِيها لا يُخَفَّفُ عَنْهُمُ الْعَذابُ وَلا هُمْ يُنْظَرُونَ (88)","quran_en":"87. Their recompense is that upon them rests the curse of God, the angels and all people, 88. abiding therein. Their punishment will not be lightened, nor will they be granted respite.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The referent of “abiding therein”","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Their”—those who have renounced Islam—“recompense” is that the angels and all people curse them, believers and unbelievers alike. For the unbeliever curses whoever abandons the true religion, even if he does not recognize who actually follows the truth. They abide in the curse; or in the Fire, which the context indicates; or in the punishment. Their punishment is not lightened for an hour, nor are they given a moment’s respite from it.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Their”—those who have renounced Islam—“recompense” is that the angels and all people curse them, believers and unbelievers alike. For the unbeliever curses whoever abandons the true religion, even if he does not recognize who actually follows the truth. They abide in the curse; or in the Fire, which the context indicates; or in the punishment. Their punishment is not lightened for an hour, nor are they given a moment’s respite from it."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Then al-Ḥārith regretted what he had done and sent to his people, asking them to ask the Messenger, blessings and peace be upon him, whether repentance was open to him. Thereupon the following words of God Most High were revealed:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Then al-Ḥārith regretted what he had done and sent to his people, asking them to ask the Messenger, blessings and peace be upon him, whether repentance was open to him. Thereupon the following words of God Most High were revealed:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":710,"arabic_characters":597}];
